        ":question/id": "c86eaeec-6ebb-5ca2-b873-ed22e534b43c",
        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=x^{3} + 1$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int 2^{x^{3} + 1} x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x^{3} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(3 x^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 2^{x^{3} + 1} x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int 2^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int 2^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "46259390-3467-5d90-82d3-5fa6678d6d96",
