        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 2^{2 - 2 x^{2}} x\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int 2^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{2^{u}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 - 2 x^{2}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(- 4 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 2^{2 - 2 x^{2}} x\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int 2^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int 2^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{2^{u}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{2^{2 - 2 x^{2}}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)+C.\n$$",
